steps.body:
Two keywords change how a loop runs:

- `break` exits the loop immediately. Nothing else in the loop runs, not even the update.
- `continue` skips the rest of the current pass and jumps straight to the update, then the condition check.

`break` is how you search: stop at the first match instead of checking everything else. Since the loop variable vanishes after the loop, save the answer in a variable declared before it. Start it at a value that means "not found", such as `-1`.

```javascript
let found = -1;
for (let i = 1; i <= 50; i++) {
  if (i % 7 === 0) {
    found = i;
    break;
  }
}
console.log(found); // 7
```

`continue` filters: put the skip test at the top of the body and the normal work below it. This keeps the main code unindented.

Gotcha: `break` only leaves the innermost loop it sits in, and inside a `switch` it leaves the switch, not the loop.

example.code:
let first = -1;
for (let i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
  if (i % 6 === 0 && i % 8 === 0) {
    first = i;
    break;
  }
}
console.log(`First: ${first}`);

for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  if (i % 3 === 0) {
    continue;
  }
  console.log(i);
}

